<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light"{% if home %} data-home{% endif %}>
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title | safe }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ description | safe }}">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical }}" />
{%- if home %}
<!-- Markdown surface for LLMs / coding agents. GitHub Pages can't emit an HTTP
     Link header, so this tag is the only alternate advertisement available. -->
<link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" href="/llms.txt" title="LLM-friendly Markdown overview" />
{%- endif %}

<!-- Open Graph / Twitter. Card art: assets/og.png -- regenerate from og.html (#v1) when the tagline changes. -->
<meta property="og:type" content="website" />
<meta property="og:site_name" content="Steer" />
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ ogTitle | default(title) | safe }}" />
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ ogDescription | default(description) | safe }}" />
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical }}" />
<meta property="og:image" content="https://steer.seanfloyd.dev/assets/og.png" />
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
<meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="The Steer app icon beside the wordmark Steer and the line: Your whole Mac, from a controller." />
<meta property="og:locale" content="en_CA" />
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ twitterTitle | default(ogTitle | default(title)) | safe }}" />
<meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ twitterDescription | default(ogDescription | default(description)) | safe }}" />
<meta name="twitter:image" content="https://steer.seanfloyd.dev/assets/og.png" />
<!-- theme init in <head> so the page paints in the right appearance on first frame (no light flash on a dark Mac) -->
{#- Authored as a real file, pasted in here, exactly like the SVG in
    _includes/art/. It has to be INLINE in the output -- it must set data-theme
    before the first frame or a dark Mac gets a white flash, and an external
    file is a round trip in front of that frame -- but there is no reason for it
    to be AUTHORED here. Living in this template meant nothing read it: eslint
    globs home.js and every .mjs and cannot see JavaScript inside a .njk, so
    this was the last unlinted script in the repo.

    Nunjucks `include` does the pasting, the same mechanism the art files use.
    No plugin: eleventy-plugin-bundle exists and is first-party, but it solves
    collecting per-page JS into a bundle, which is not this.

    The homepage reads one key more than the rest of the site: the accent picker
    in the Feel band writes steer-accent, and the [data-accent] token overrides
    it selects live in styles/tokens.css, which every page loads. Sub-pages
    deliberately do not restore it, so the accent stays a homepage treatment
    rather than a sitewide preference. That used to be a {% if home %} wrapped
    around the JS itself, which is what stopped the script being a plain file;
    the flag is `data-home` on <html> now and the script reads the DOM, so the
    file is ordinary JavaScript a linter can parse.

    It also validates the stored value against light|dark before using it.
    Without that clause a stored "SYSTEM" became data-theme="SYSTEM" (matching
    no rule), the label read "Theme: undefined", and the first pick wrote the
    literal string "undefined" into storage, leaving the control that exists to
    recover the state permanently dead. #}
<script>{% include "scripts/theme-init.js" %}</script>
<link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="assets/mark.png">
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="apple-touch-icon.png">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="site.css">
{%- if home %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="home.css">
{%- endif %}
{#- A page's own sheet, styles/pages/<page>.css, bundled to dist/<page>.css.
    Same derivable pairing a band has, and the same reason: five pages used to
    carry a <style> block in their front matter and the rest pushed one-page
    rules into shared.css, which ships on all 14. Linked last and unlayered,
    exactly where those <style> blocks sat, so the cascade is unchanged. #}
{%- if pageName in pageSheets %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ pageName }}.css">
{%- endif %}
{%- if robots %}
<meta name="robots" content="{{ robots }}">
{%- endif %}
{%- if head %}
{{ head | safe }}
{%- endif %}
</head>
<body>
<div class="llm-only" aria-hidden="true">An LLM-friendly Markdown overview of Steer is available at https://steer.seanfloyd.dev/llms.txt</div>
{% include "chrome/nav.html" %}
{{ content | safe }}
{% include "chrome/footer.html" %}
{%- if home -%}
{% include "bands/dialogs.html" %}
<script src="home.js" defer></script>
{%- endif %}
{#- The appearance control's behaviour is theme.js, loaded on every page. It is
    a file and not an inline block here for the reason its own header gives:
    eslint reads home.js and every .mjs and reads nothing inside a .njk, so this
    logic was unlinted for as long as it lived in this file.

    The <head> script above is the half that cannot move -- it must set
    data-theme before the first frame or a dark Mac gets a white flash, and an
    external file is a round trip in front of that frame. It also validates the
    stored value against light|dark before using it: without that clause a
    stored "SYSTEM" became data-theme="SYSTEM" (matching no rule), the label
    read "Theme: undefined", and the first pick wrote the literal string
    "undefined" into storage, leaving the control that exists to recover the
    state permanently dead. Nothing here writes such a value, so it was
    unreachable rather than live, and one clause is cheaper than trusting it
    stays unreachable. #}
<script src="theme.js" defer></script>
{%- if bodyEnd %}
{{ bodyEnd | safe }}
{%- endif %}
</body>
</html>
